      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14151706/assets/36008a9155a01a07136cee263ce0a89239719227541536e8c2f8ea358d43e6df.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\cancel{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\cancel{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
